# iFive mini 4S — Android 16, {{DATE}}

- System: {{SYSTEM}}, with the ASUS Tinker Board Android 11 vendor partition and this project's changes.
- Kernel: Linux 6.1 (Rockchip develop-6.1). Graphics buffers come from dma-buf heaps.
- Boot partition: {{BOOT}}.

## What you need

A Linux x86_64 PC and a USB cable. Everything else is in this folder: `rkdeveloptool` is in `tools/`.

Check the download first:

```sh
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
```

## Download mode

Power the tablet off. Hold Volume Down and plug in USB.

```sh
sudo tools/rkdeveloptool ld      # prints Loader or Maskrom
```

`sudo` is there because a normal user can't open the USB device without a udev rule for `2207:320a`. The same goes for every command below.

## Coming from stock Android 6

```sh
sudo bash restore.sh
```

The stock partition table gives `system` 1536 MiB. This system needs 3072 MiB, and everything after `system` moves. `restore.sh` writes the new partition table, then all images. It asks you to type `restore` before it writes anything.

## Coming from an earlier build of this project

```sh
sudo bash flash.sh --yes                   # wipes your data
sudo bash flash.sh --yes --keep-userdata   # keeps your apps and data
```

`--dry-run` prints every command and writes nothing.

Coming from a build with Linux 4.19 (2026-11-15-r3 or older) and keeping data? Run `scripts/fixup-app-dirs.sh` from the repository once after the first boot (needs root: `adb root` or Magisk). 4.19 had sdcardfs, which left files in `Android/data` owned by `media_rw`; on 6.1 apps cannot open them (Obtainium shows an empty list, App Manager negative sizes). The script asks Android (vold `fixupAppDir`) to repair every app's folder. Wiping data instead also works.

Both scripts write in small pieces and read every piece back. If USB drops halfway, run the same command again and it continues where it stopped (`--fresh` starts over). The first boot takes a few minutes and shows kernel text on the screen.

## Root

Write the Magisk boot image from the same release over the boot partition:

```sh
sha256sum -c ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img.sha256
sudo tools/rkdeveloptool wl 0x16000 ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img
sudo tools/rkdeveloptool rd
```

After the first boot, run `scripts/magisk-install-data.sh Magisk-v31.0.apk` from the repository (needs `adb root`) and reboot. The Magisk app cannot finish the install itself on this tablet. Flashing again with `flash.sh` takes root away.

## Bricked

**Partition table erased, bootloader still there.** The tablet still shows up as `Loader`. `sudo bash restore.sh`. On a tablet with no partition table at all, add `--board ifive-mini4s`.

**eMMC completely blank.** The tablet shows up as `Maskrom`. In this order:

```sh
sudo bash restore.sh --board ifive-mini4s --maskrom --parameters-only
sudo bash write-loader-head.sh --board ifive-mini4s --lba-base tool
sudo bash restore.sh --board ifive-mini4s
```

The first command loads `loader/rk3288_loader_v1.12.263.bin` into RAM and writes the partition table. The second writes the bootloader (`devices/ifive-mini4s/head-4M.bin`) to the first 4 MiB of the eMMC. The third writes the system. On Windows, RKDevTool can load the same loader file and write `head-4M.bin` at address 0.

`sudo bash restore.sh --plan` prints where everything goes without touching the tablet.

## Where things go

`rkdeveloptool` counts from eMMC sector 0x2000, so tool LBA = eMMC sector − 0x2000. The bootloader lives in sectors 0x0000-0x1FFF, below tool LBA 0, which is why it has its own script.

| File | Tool LBA | Size |
|---|---|---|
| `devices/ifive-mini4s/parameter-loader.img` | 0x0000 | 4 MiB |
| `devices/ifive-mini4s/parameter-p1.img` | 0x2000 | 4 MiB |
| `images/resource.img` | 0x6000 | 16 MiB |
| `images/kernel.img` | 0xE000 | 16 MiB |
| `images/ramdisk.img` (boot) | 0x16000 | 32 MiB |
| `images/super.img` (system) | 0x92000 | 3072 MiB |
| `images/metadata.img` | 0x692000 | 16 MiB |
| `images/userdata-head-zero.img` (start of userdata) | 0x6BA000 | 1 MiB |

The scripts read these addresses from `manifest.json`, which the build makes from the partition table in `images/resource.img` and the board's parameter file. There are no addresses typed into the scripts.

## Other boards

`devices/<board>/` holds a board's partition table and bootloader. To add one, copy `devices/ifive-mini4s/`, replace `parameter-p1.img` (that board's 4 MiB p1 partition), `parameter-loader.img`, `head-4M.bin` and `head-8M.bin` (from its first 8 MiB of eMMC), and fix the names and checksums in `device.json`. `restore.sh` refuses to write if that board's partition table and this package's disagree anywhere except the size of `system`.

## Known issues

- The landscape app drawer still drops frames: launcher3 clips themed icons on the CPU. Picking the circle icon shape helps a lot.
- The tablet hangs or reboots now and then, cause not known yet. A stuck CPU makes the kernel panic, so these hangs leave a log (see below).
- After a kernel panic the tablet reboots in 5 s and the log is in `/sys/fs/pstore` (`dmesg-ramoops-*`, `console-ramoops-0`) until the next panic; read it with root, e.g. `adb shell su -c 'cat /sys/fs/pstore/dmesg-ramoops-*'`.
- Once, Wi-Fi failed to turn on for a whole boot; a reboot fixed it.
